text:
maria: Quick PSA — I walked through the Next.js starter (fresh clone) and hit a few onboarding gaps: env var mismatch, auth/retry behavior not documented, and the LangChain/LlamaIndex example still calling the old generate() API. Steps + repro below.

jake: ugh, sorry — can you paste the exact env var mismatch?

maria: in .env.example we ship NEXT_PUBLIC_REDWOOD_API_KEY but server code expects REDWOOD_API_KEY. On SSR you get a 401 because NEXT_PUBLIC_* gets inlined to the client and we lose the server secret. Minimal repro: 
```js
// pages/api/generate.js
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const key = process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY; // undefined if example var used
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Authorization': `Bearer ${key}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input: 'hi' })
  });
  res.status(r.status).send(await r.text());
}
```

samir: also saw flaky eval harness runs — some tests compare whole response strings but the SDK streams tokens for some models, so CI is non-deterministic when streaming is enabled. failing snippet: 
```
AssertionError: expected full_string to equal snapshot
Received partial tokens on first read
```

emily: that sounds like two separate DX problems: 1) docs/sample mismatch (env + server vs client), 2) SDK parity/behavior difference causing eval flakes. JS SDK has responses.stream, python sample still uses .generate; we should normalise examples to the new "responses" surface.

jake: agree. plan: update .env.example to REDWOOD_API_KEY, add a clear "server vs client" note in README, and change LlamaIndex/LangChain examples to use responses.create (or the shim) so both py/js are consistent. I'll open a PR.

maria: also think we should add an explicit small retry helper in the sample for transient 401/429s while onboarding — newbies copy paste the sample and get confused. Example helper: 
```ts
// samples/utils/retry.ts
export async function withRetries(fn, retries = 2) {
  let i = 0;
  while (true) {
    try { return await fn(); }
    catch (e) { if (i++ >= retries) throw e; await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 200 * i)); }
  }
}
```

jake: makes sense — we can include that as a tiny opt-in util and reference it in the README under "recommended retries for onboarding".

devex-bot: created issue #438 in https://github.com/redwood-inference/samples (samples/nextjs-starter): "env var mismatch + SDK parity + eval flakiness" — assigned to @jake.

samir: for the eval harness, quick mitigation: add a --no-stream flag so CI always runs deterministic non-streamed captures, and add a small retry wrapper around the harness runner to tolerate transient model timeouts. I can draft the change.

emily: perfect. also we should add a tiny note in the LangChain/LlamaIndex readme about which SDK versions map to which method names (generate -> responses.create) to reduce churn as we roll out new surfaces.

jake: I'll open a single PR that: 1) fixes .env.example, 2) adds the retry util, 3) updates the LangChain/LlamaIndex sample adapters to the new API and includes compatibility shim, 4) adds --no-stream flag to eval harness and a CI toggle. pinging @emily and @samir for review.

maria: thank you! can you also add a short "onboarding checklist" in the repo root README for "first-run gotchas" (env var, local dev token rotation, SSR note)? saves time for new users.

jake: will do. any objections to making REDWOOD_API_KEY the canonical name? :thumbsup:

maria: none. thanks team.

emily: will review the PR today once opened.

samir: i'll draft the eval changes and link them in the PR.

jake: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/samples/pull/612 — thanks all, will iterate on feedback.

maria: awesome, closing the loop. leaving a quick blurb for docs: "If you hit 401 on first-run: check env var name (server vs client) and ensure you are not exposing secrets to the client)."
